Syntax for the if statement in PHP:
if (condition)
{
// code to be executed if condition is true;
}
The condition is enclosed in parentheses ( ). The code block is enclosed in curly braces { }. The code inside the curly braces is executed only when the condition is true. If the condition is false, the code block is skipped entirely.
Example: $x = 10; if ($x > 5) { echo "x is greater than 5"; } Output: x is greater than 5

Syntax for the if...else statement in PHP:
if (condition)
{
// True-block — code executed if condition is true;
}
else
{
// False-block — code executed if condition is false;
}
If the condition is TRUE, the True-block executes and the False-block is skipped. If the condition is FALSE, the False-block executes and the True-block is skipped. Exactly one of the two blocks always executes.

Syntax:
if (condition1)
{
// code executed if condition1 is true;
}
elseif (condition2)
{
// code executed if condition1 is false and condition2 is true;
}
elseif (condition3)
{
// code executed if condition1 and condition2 are false and condition3 is true;
}
else
{
// code executed if all conditions are false;
}
Key rules: Only ONE block executes — the first true condition wins. The else block at the end handles all remaining cases. elseif can also be written as 'else if' (two words). You can have as many elseif blocks as needed.

Syntax:
switch (expression)
{
case value1:
//code executed if expression equals value1;
break;
case value2:
//code executed if expression equals value2;
break;
...
default:
//code executed if expression matches no case;
}
Key points: The expression is evaluated once. Each case checks if the expression equals its value. break terminates the case and exits the switch. default is optional — it runs when no case matches.

The if...elseif...else statement allows checking multiple conditions and running different code based on which condition is true.

Syntax:
if (condition1) { // runs if condition1 is true }
elseif (condition2) { // runs if condition1 false and condition2 true }
elseif (condition3) { // runs if condition1 and condition2 false and condition3 true }
else { // runs if all conditions are false }

Key rules:
1. Starts with 'if', followed by one or more 'elseif', ends with 'else'.
2. Only ONE block executes — the first condition that is true.
3. If no condition is true, the else block runs.
4. elseif can also be written as two words: else if.

Example:
<?php
$x = 12;
if ($x > 20)
{
echo "x is greater than 20";
}
elseif ($x > 15)
{
echo "x is greater than 15 but not 20";
}
elseif ($x > 10)
{
echo "x is greater than 10 but not 15";
}
else
{
echo "x is not greater than 10";
}
?>

Output: x is greater than 10 but not greater than 15
(Because 12 > 20 is false, 12 > 15 is false, 12 > 10 is true — so the third block runs.)

The switch statement is a multiple branching statement where, based on a condition, control transfers to one of many possible points. It is used to specify multiple conditions for one variable.

Syntax:
switch (expression)
{
case value1:
//code if expression = value1;
break;
case value2:
//code if expression = value2;
break;
default:
//code if no case matches;
}

How it works:
1. The expression is evaluated once.
2. Each case value is compared to the expression.
3. When a matching case is found, its code block executes.
4. break terminates the case and exits the switch.
5. If no case matches, the default block executes (optional).
6. Without break, execution falls through to the next case.

Example:
<?php
$x = 10;
switch ($x)
{
case 5:
echo "x is equal to 5";
break;
case 10:
echo "x is equal to 10";
break;
case 15:
echo "x is equal to 15";
break;
default:
echo "x is not equal to 5, 10, or 15";
}
?>

Output: x is equal to 10
(Because $x = 10 matches case 10. break exits the switch after printing.)

Importance of break: Without break, after case 10 prints, case 15 would also execute. Always use break to prevent fall-through.
